- The internal test file is properly justified (needs `w.(*jobWatcher).podLister`) and mirrors the repo's established internal/external precedent; the suite bootstrap (`TestPkg`/`RunSpecs`) will pick up the new specs.
- Container-executable verification is honest (no exit-code-masked operator commands) and the prompt proactively bans `docker`/`kubectl`/`dark-factory` from itself.
- YAGNI-clean: no scope creep beyond the spec's Goal, and non-goals (sweeper fallback, memory limit, permissions) are explicitly load-bearing.

## Summary
An exemplary, fully verified prompt: every code reference checks out against source, the internal-test design compiles mentally, failure modes and ACs are exhaustively covered, and all container-autonomy/hideGit/operator-only gates pass. The only substantive finding is a Recommendation — the unmentioned `HandlePod`/dedupe ordering race that can shadow the new `pod_oom_killed` value in production — worth a one-line acknowledgment in the prompt before approval. Priority action: apply Recommendation 1 (and optionally 2), then approve.
